{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-activity eviction causes (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(o)-(r))","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or who pleads guilty to, assault, terrorist threats, theft or human trafficking, or who knowingly houses such a person. A three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order for listed causes. Notice is three months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month, or three days for disorderly conduct.","key_value":"3-month / 1-month / 3-day notice","coverage_conditions":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before eviction.","key_value":"At least 3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"units","role":"covered","op":"at_least","n":2}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house if it does not meet one (1) of the exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act"]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere; school and college dormitories; buildings housing only college students; certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (owner occupancy and demolition)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it at least six months, and one who evicts to permanently board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; treble damages; civil penalty up to $10,000","coverage_conditions":null,"applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty of up to $10,000 per violation.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the  landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter the lease, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal for a tenant enforcing lease or legal rights, complaining to a government authority, joining a tenant organization, or refusing retaliatory lease changes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure notice to quit and change-of-ownership notice","requirement":"Before evicting a tenant because of foreclosure, a buyer who wants to personally occupy the home must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a person acquiring a foreclosed property must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; notices within 10 business days","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Foreclosure as the triggering event; the 90-day notice applies where a buyer wants to personally occupy the property as a primary residence.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":null,"relations":[{"type":"yields_to_local","quote":"the federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants  in foreclosure proceedings"}],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans, and who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee-simple ownership at least one year before recording, may be protected from eviction for 40 years.","key_value":"40 years; income no more than 3 times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants meeting the age, disability or veteran criteria who lived in the building being converted at least one year before the conversion recording date; income limit applies.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant age (62+), permanent disability or qualifying disabled-veteran status, at least one year of residency before the conversion recording date, and family income within the limit (up to 3 times the county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater).","coverage_quotes":["To qualify, tenants must :  (1) be at least 62 years of age before the d ate of the conversion recording of the condominium or cooperative; or (2) be permanently disabled; or (3)  be  honorably discharged from any military service under certain circumstances from any branch of the U.S. Armed Forces and disabled at 60% or higher resulting from said service and, (4) live in a building being converted to a condominium, cooperative; or fee simple ownership of units at least one (1) year prior to the conversion recording date."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County)","requirement":"Extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; currently only Hudson County.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenants in a qualified county (currently only Hudson County) in a building converted or being converted who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","coverage_quotes":["The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981”"]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
